<h2 class="mt-14">Consent and purchaser suppression for Segmentation</h2>
<p>For a lifecycle marketer, the important distinction is segment membership versus send eligibility. A customer can belong to a product-interest group while being ineligible for a promotion. Evaluate the message purpose, channel permission, current subscription state, and exclusions again at dispatch; a saved audience is not permission to send.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Audience state</th><th>Required handling</th><th>Rule to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Declared category preference</td><td>Use the stated interest for relevant content; allow updates</td><td>A preference change removes old queued category promotions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recent matching-product purchase</td><td>Cancel obsolete acquisition reminders for that product</td><td>Purchase after audience creation but before dispatch exits the reminder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unrelated purchase</td><td>Reassess relevance and combined frequency rather than suppress all marketing automatically</td><td>Buying shoes does not erase an explicit interest in coats</td></tr>
<tr><td>Open return or support case</td><td>Pause conflicting promotions; continue required service updates</td><td>Case closure triggers a fresh eligibility check, not replay of the old queue</td></tr>
<tr><td>No evidenced marketing permission</td><td>Exclude from that promotional channel pending resolution</td><td>Order-service eligibility does not subscribe a customer to marketing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Email permission only</td><td>Keep the customer out of SMS promotions</td><td>Channel fields do not inherit permission from a general audience label</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeated non-engagement</td><td>Review clicks, purchases, delivery and preference history before reducing frequency</td><td>Open tracking alone does not decide inactivity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Free or disposable domain</td><td>Assess address validity and actual abuse signals</td><td>A free mailbox alone does not imply invalid consent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wholesale account</td><td>Separate trade terms and consumer offers where relevant</td><td>Account-level changes reach each eligible contact without duplicate sends</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff or test profile</td><td>Exclude from customer reporting and production campaigns</td><td>QA profiles exercise exclusions without polluting experiment outcomes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy imported profile</td><td>Resolve permission provenance and field meaning before promotional use</td><td>Importing a tag does not create consent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delayed or replayed event</td><td>Reconcile current state and deduplicate by event and journey</td><td>An older event cannot restore an obsolete segment membership</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="mt-12">Margin, app costs, and pricing for Segmentation</h2>
<p>For segmentation, revenue attribution flatters; margin decides. Burden the segmentation program with its true load — platform fees, overages, SMS, capture, creative, agency, and segmentation discount cost — before calling it profitable. For segmentation, a stack at fifteen percent of attributed margin needs simplification first, optimization second.</p>
<p>Pricing changes frequently and varies by region, contact volume, and contract term, so check the official pricing pages of every shortlisted app and model an eighteen-month total that includes a peak season. Segmentation free tiers look generous until one limit bites — verify which of contacts, sends, branching, or support constrains segmentation first.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost component</th><th>What to model</th><th>Common failure</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Platform subscription</td><td>Segmentation tier matched to true contact volume</td><td>Segmentation tier bought for phantom growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contact or send overages</td><td>Segmentation growth pace versus plan ceilings</td><td>Peak segmentation volume blowing past tier limits</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS credits</td><td>Segmentation text volume from real opt-in math</td><td>Treating segmentation texts like near-free email</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discount budget</td><td>Segmentation discount load from depth and uptake</td><td>segmentation flows teaching shoppers to hold out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Creative and ops time</td><td>Weekly segmentation hours to run and QA flows</td><td>Segmentation edit and QA effort undercounted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Migration and setup</td><td>segmentation migration: data, consent maps, rebuilds</td><td>Segmentation consent lost mid-migration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support and success tiers</td><td>Segmentation support tier for critical incidents</td><td>segmentation support shortfall during the worst week</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third-party integrations</td><td>Segmentation satellite fees beyond the ESP</td><td>Creeping segmentation add-ons dwarfing ESP spend</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deliverability remediation</td><td>Segmentation monitoring, cleaning, advisory spend</td><td>Segmentation inbox harm priced above the plan</td></tr>
